  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on the specific monocentric structure and historical institutional context of Shanghai, which may limit direct generalizability to cities with different governance models.",
      "what": "This study analyzes urban segregation in Shanghai using high-resolution data on housing tenure, housing costs, real-time commuting times, and job opportunities. It maps a socio-spatial division of mobility across three distinct zones: a privileged central zone, a comfortable suburban zone, and a marginalized outer zone.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that urban segregation is not merely a product of individual choices or static residential locations, but is actively produced and maintained by historically institutionalized urban governance that prioritizes strategic central zones over marginalized populations."
